    1 2ABB              ; runtime.asm -- what the 8080 does not have: multiplication, division and the shifts by a
    2 2ABB              ; variable count, sixteen bits and thirty-two.
    3 2ABB              ;
    4 2ABB              ; The compiler expands what it can inline and calls in here for the rest, under the ordinary
    5 2ABB              ; compiler's calling convention: a sixteen-bit operation takes its operands in HL and DE
    6 2ABB              ; and returns in HL; a thirty-two-bit one takes the first operand's low word in HL and high word
    7 2ABB              ; in DE and the second's in BC and on the stack above the return address (or a count in BC), and
    8 2ABB              ; returns in HL (low) and DE (high). So these are ordinary functions as far as the compiler is
    9 2ABB              ; concerned.
   10 2ABB              ;
   11 2ABB              ; Only what a program uses is assembled: every routine is inside IFUSED, so an image carries the
   12 2ABB              ; routines it calls and the ones those call, and nothing else. sjasmplus decides that in one pass
   13 2ABB              ; only if every routine comes after all the routines that call it -- the order below -- which is
   14 2ABB              ; also why the thirty-two-bit routines, which call the sixteen-bit ones, come first. They used to
   15 2ABB              ; be in runtime32.asm; that file is now empty and kept so that command lines naming it still work.
   16 2ABB              ;
   17 2ABB              ; Division of longs keeps its operands in static memory, because it needs a dividend, a divisor
   18 2ABB              ; and a remainder of four bytes each and the machine has three pairs, and so does the floating
   19 2ABB              ; point. An interrupt handler gets a copy of its own of this whole file, every name of it NAME__i,
   20 2ABB              ; which x80cc makes when it links a program whose handlers call into here: the handler never runs
   21 2ABB              ; inside the program's routines, nor in their working space. What the two share is between a line ";@shared" and one ";@end", which the
   22 2ABB              ; copy leaves out.
   23 2ABB              ;
   24 2ABB              ; Sixty-four-bit arithmetic takes its operands by address, the result written over the first
   25 2ABB              ; operand, since eight bytes fit in no set of registers; see the comment above those routines.
   26 2ABB              ;
   27 2ABB              ; Written by hand and measured rather than guessed at: every routine is checked against the same
   28 2ABB              ; operations computed in C by the compiler's own interpreter.
   29 2ABB
